  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "review-3227f7e23574-1-review-1",
      "kind": "blocking",
      "status": "resolved",
      "acceptance_id": "A1",
      "source": "review",
      "review_round": 1,
      "summary": "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:156-158 procEnviron swallows every read error, so a process that cannot be checked is treated as not carrying the token. If an enumerable live detached descendant's environ read fails with EIO or any unexpected error, scanTokenPids still returns a success set, killTree can confirm cleanup while that descendant is alive, and the check returns exit_code 0; the generated bundle carries the same defect. Distinguish an expected vanished process (ENOENT) and explicitly permitted access refusals from unknown read errors, let unknown errors propagate into the scan-failure path, and add a test that a readable process table whose single environ read fails must refuse success.",
      "anchor": "sha256:3759967201dfb3dfe759b91845c0057c75113a473796d21e7bd278d85263897a",
      "evidence": {
        "trigger": "On Linux a verification parent exits successfully, leaving a live detached descendant that inherited the token and closed its inherited pipes; readdirSync(procRoot) succeeds and includes that PID, but reading its environ throws EIO. The error is converted to null, so the PID is never added to the cleanup set.",
        "caller_chain": [
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:runFixedVerification.finish",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:runFixedVerification.killTree",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:runFixedVerification.scanTokenPids",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:runFixedVerification.procEnviron"
        ],
        "violated": {
          "kind": "security_boundary",
          "ref": "verification-cleanup-before-success"
        }
      },
      "counterevidence": null
    },
    {
      "id": "review-26e0ecff4818-1-review-1",
      "kind": "blocking",
      "status": "resolved",
      "acceptance_id": "A1",
      "source": "review",
      "review_round": 2,
      "summary": "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:175-178 reads environ for every numeric PID unconditionally, so verification on an ordinary non-root Linux host is blocked by unrelated privileged processes: /proc/1/environ returns EACCES, the whole scan returns null, and VerificationCleanupError is thrown even when the command succeeded and left nothing behind; an existing detached token carrier also never reaches signalPids because the scan failed first. Narrow the candidate set by verifiable process ownership first, exclude definitively unrelated privileged processes, keep failing closed for real candidates and an unreadable proc root, add tests holding both an unrelated unreadable process and a readable token carrier, and resync the generated bundle.",
      "anchor": "sha256:2b80086112f5a8e35b4baeae8d0a904705606ffeca4488f27ec5e5d5c4cf9afe",
      "evidence": {
        "trigger": "An unprivileged user runs a successful command on Linux with the default /proc, where the table contains root-owned PID 1 whose environ returns EACCES. The scan therefore returns null and VerificationCleanupError is thrown even though the command succeeded and nothing was left behind; if the command also had a detached descendant, the failed scan additionally prevents that descendant from being signalled.",
        "caller_chain": [
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:runFixedVerification",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:finish",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:killTree",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:scanTokenPids",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:procEnviron"
        ],
        "violated": {
          "kind": "acceptance",
          "ref": "A1"
        }
      },
      "counterevidence": null
    },
    {
      "id": "review-3600f28f26fc-1-review-1",
      "kind": "blocking",
      "status": "resolved",
      "acceptance_id": "A1",
      "source": "review",
      "review_round": 3,
      "summary": "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:199 excludes processes by current real UID, which cannot prove they did not inherit the token. When verification runs as root, a detached descendant can keep the environment, drop its UID and continue running; the scan skips it, killTree then returns success and a process that can still mutate the verification outputs is left behind. At minimum, inspect other UIDs' environments whenever the host grants cross-UID reads, never treat a different UID alone as proof of irrelevance, add a cleanup test for a token-retaining descendant that dropped privileges, and resync the generated bundle.",
      "anchor": "sha256:8557d4b4247bfd3547b17864d935b334d19695dd831327e068b0eabe0bf5d5b0",
      "evidence": {
        "trigger": "A fixed verification command runs as root on Linux and creates a detached descendant that keeps IMM_VERIFICATION_PROCESS_TOKEN in its environment, calls setuid to a different uid and keeps running; the direct child exits successfully. scanTokenPids skips that entry because its uid differs, so killTree sees no survivor and confirms cleanup while the token carrier is still alive.",
        "caller_chain": [
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:runFixedVerification",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:finish",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:killTree",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:scanTokenPids"
        ],
        "violated": {
          "kind": "acceptance",
          "ref": "A1"
        }
      },
      "counterevidence": null
    },
    {
      "id": "review-783743403bc3-1-review-1",
      "kind": "blocking",
      "status": "resolved",
      "acceptance_id": "A1",
      "source": "review",
      "review_round": 4,
      "summary": "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:204-207 skips any process whose uid differs when its environ cannot be read, so cleanup can be confirmed while a token-carrying detached descendant is still running: on Linux a root run that holds CAP_SETUID but lacks CAP_SYS_PTRACE can spawn a descendant that drops its uid, whose environ read is refused, yet which the scan treats as unrelated. Keep trustworthy attribution evidence for cross-uid descendants and refuse a confirmed cleanup when their ownership cannot be excluded; add the matching regression test and resync the generated bundle. The existing synthetic tests only cover a cross-uid carrier whose environ is readable, so they cannot protect this path.",
      "anchor": "sha256:e56d76b2cdf5e689ca4e51ef6c298f6a55868e69a2c0d50422a810373a24833b",
      "evidence": {
        "trigger": "A Linux host runs the fixed verification command as UID 0 with CAP_SETUID but without CAP_SYS_PTRACE; the command creates a detached descendant that inherits the token, calls setuid to another UID and keeps running while the parent exits successfully. Reading that descendant's status succeeds, but reading its environ returns EACCES, so the scan skips it and cleanup is confirmed.",
        "caller_chain": [
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:runFixedVerification",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:finish",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:killTree",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:scanTokenPids",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:procEnviron"
        ],
        "violated": {
          "kind": "security_boundary",
          "ref": "verification-process-cleanup-proof"
        }
      },
      "counterevidence": null
    },
    {
      "id": "review-7e0684a5c500-1-review-1",
      "kind": "blocking",
      "status": "resolved",
      "acceptance_id": "A1",
      "source": "review",
      "review_round": 5,
      "summary": "[P1] plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:176 treats a legitimate session 0 as a read failure. On an ordinary Linux host with visible kernel threads, processes such as kthreadd report session 0 in /proc/<pid>/stat, so every scan aborts at line 188 and even a successfully exited check ends in VerificationCleanupError. Accept a non-negative session, handle 0 as a session that is not this run's, add a synthetic procfs regression test containing a session 0 entry, and resync the generated bundle. The current fixtures only cover positive sessions, so a green QA cannot rule this defect out.",
      "anchor": "sha256:7ce06c04acc85bac2482db891094110738f093a78ab8afc02654ce8efe245758",
      "evidence": {
        "trigger": "A Linux host's procfs contains legitimate session 0 processes, for example /proc/2/stat for kthreadd, and any verification command reaches cleanup after exiting.",
        "caller_chain": [
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:runFixedVerification",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:finish",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/assurance/verification.ts:killTree",
